### Section 182.2

Database persistence strategies dictate the overall responsiveness of desktop clients. Embedded solutions like SQLite paired with Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) and memory temp stores provide near-zero overhead without network latency penalties.

| Key | Value | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| LRU_MAX | 25 | Maximum entries retained |
| RAM_BUDGET | 8MB | Hard memory ceiling |
| CACHE_HIT | 0ms | Instant retrieval |

### Section 182.3

ProseMirror and Tiptap represent structured document editors maintaining synchronized DOM representations, transactional history trees, and immutable document state nodes. Recreating editor instances on note switches causes noticeable layout reflows and memory thrashing.

```typescript
function processItem<T>(item: T): Promise<T> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve(item), 16);
  });
}
```

### Section 182.4

Memory caching strategies using Least Recently Used (LRU) algorithms bound client footprint while delivering instantaneous retrieval for frequently navigated resources. Tracking both entry counts and byte budgets prevents pathological out-of-memory states.

